{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793459320,"nonce":"q6m1eTpbIKFIB4oH93BXN307hKE=","message":"Implemented in the commit above (local, not pushed): std/src/offload.rs runs a blocking closure on a pool of OS threads while the calling fiber is parked (rt::new_waiter / park / wake), so its scheduler thread keeps running other fibers. Used for process stdout/stderr read, stdin write, the blocking exit wait, and Sys.stdin reads.\n\nEvidence (release build, fixtures TestProcessPipeFibers and TestStdinFibers, a blocked reader and a ticking sibling thread, ASH_WORKERS=0): the 0.1.14-era nightly starves or hangs in interp, hybrid and jit; the new build runs the sibling in all three, and a stdin reader no longer holds the run until the pipe closes (9 s before, 0 s after). The debug-build tests in crates/ash_cli/tests/process_pipe_gc.rs pass in interp, hybrid and jit. Unit conformance 1350/1350 under isolation; wasm runtimes build.\n\nNot covered: sockets, Sys.stdout/stderr writes (still bracketed with hl_blocking only, because offloading every println adds a thread hop), and a non-blocking exit/pid call still waits on the child lock while another fiber is in a blocking exit. Whether this changes RayDE's frame rate is not measured.","files":null}]}